@weaverbot, can we rescope this issue and the possible fixes to be a bit more contained to start.
1. Evaluate any place that we're manually settings `MARIN_PREFIX` in code. Why is it there and can we remove it? As rjpower says, that seems like a code smell.
2. Can we do something simpler to start that is like `check_path_in_region`? It would just analyze the inputs (e.g. the `region` flag, `MARIN_PREFIX` and anything else that would determine where the workers will run) and throws unless an override is present?

@ravwojdyla This looks fun! I've been doing a bunch of work on Arrow C++/PyArrow/Parquet to make tensor work feasible (e.g. [FixedShapeTensorArray](https://arrow.apache.org/docs/python/generated/pyarrow.FixedShapeTensorArray.html), [Vector in Parquet](https://gist.github.com/rok/fe4785d4a74d2e080cbad73e88cc1bef)) and I'd be quite interested in helping on Arrow side of things so as to make sure we're positioning in the right direction where it can be more useful to ML projects.

To the discussion here - Polars has an enterprise [distributed engine](https://docs.pola.rs/polars-cloud/run/distributed-engine/) which would mean it probably composes nicely into a distributed system, but on the other hand it will probably always be missing things from the community offering that would be useful for building a distributed query engine due to the business plan.

[Apache Comet](https://datafusion.apache.org/comet/) is buit on top of DataFusion and could perhaps be interesting?

> That said, a somewhat crazy option to have a Rust / Python shuffle engine (which would work for sql via DataFusion) would be to use the https://github.com/marin-community/marin/pull/5963 and push the shuffle down into Polars via [map_groups](https://docs.pola.rs/api/python/stable/reference/dataframe/api/polars.dataframe.group_by.GroupBy.map_groups.html#polars.dataframe.group_by.GroupBy.map_groups).

@wmoss that sounds interesting! I have no opinion on polars, as long as there was a reusable and cleanly scoped component that could work for both zephyr and SQ - I'm all for it. And if that's just something that we extract from zephyr, even better? Maybe that's another reason to push for #5963?

<hr>

@rok we should definitely keep in touch on Arrow, we use it in a couple of places. Unrelated to this issue, we currently use tensorstore for final training token storage (write once, read ranges), at some point I was curious if I could replace that with Arrow/Parquet, do you happen to have experience with that use-case? Scale ~20T. Actually, https://github.com/marin-community/marin/issues/4445 would be a good place to have this chat to keep this issue focused.

Since now we have an expert in the house (👋 @gabotechs), I'm curious to understand more about how something like DataFusion-Distributed could work well with preemption. It seems like the design (specifically no checkpointing) means that if a node dies, you have to retry the entire query. In a system with preemption, as you increase time and node count (e.g. make your data size larger) your query success rate would go to zero.

The only solution I know of to that problem is checkpointing, but DataFusion-Distributed seems to be making the decision to not do that in the interest of speed (which is a great decision IMO, just not for us, since we can't reliably keep nodes up).

@alxmrs have you tried doing some simple processing on something like the 3 PiB dataset with Zephyr? Does it work? It would be interesting just to understand if we have a working solution at all.

@alxmrs just to make sure I'm understanding the many threads we have in this issue now (😅), you're suggesting Zarr / Tensorstore for the tokenized data (which is array-shaped) but not any of the intermediate or input representations?

@claude can you look at how we could deploy Ballista with Iris? We'd probably want to do something similar to Zephyr where we deploy both the scheduler and N executors per query. I'm specifically interested in how Ballista handles node failure. If an executor nodes fails and Iris brings up another one, what happens? If the schedule fails, does the entire query fail or can it resume?

@wmoss agreed! Two thoughts on direction:
 * definitely agreed on ducky! We should make sure there's a cheap and reasonable way to keep ballista warm? Having it warm would be best case scenario, but it may be a nice-to-have(?), anyways I trust you can find the optimal way.
 * on the zephyr - could a reasonable first step be to introduce an explicit `.sql`, it would just break the fusion (at least to start). We could have the ballista warm and collocated on zephyr workers.

