@weaverbot I've merged https://github.com/marin-community/marin/pull/8458 and run all the steps to manually import the new IAM grants to the marin gcp stack and remove the IAM grants from echo, grafana and evaldash. Can you create a PR that,
1. Removes the `IAM_STATE_TRANSFER_BLOCKED_ROLLOUTS` code added in https://github.com/marin-community/marin/pull/8458 since those pulumi stacks are okay now and ready to return to autodeploys.
3. Makes IAM authoritative. You'll need to check IAM one last time to make sure we're not going to clobber any state drift that's happened since the import in https://github.com/marin-community/marin/pull/8458. If there is state drift, just put up a PR removing `IAM_STATE_TRANSFER_BLOCKED_ROLLOUTS`.

🤖 Implemented in [PR #8503](https://github.com/marin-community/marin/pull/8503).

The IaC Preview workflow now:

- starts a deployment check only for merged, same-repository Pulumi PRs;
- waits 30 minutes, then retries after 60 and 120 minutes when needed;
- previews the PR merge commit so a newer, un-applied merge does not trigger an older PR reminder;
- suppresses historical diffs when a successful stack update completed after the PR merged; and
- mentions the merger in a sticky PR comment when changes remain or a preview fails.

The workflow reuses the existing read-only Pulumi identity and never applies infrastructure changes. CI is green across 41 checks, and the affected safe test selection passed all 8 tests. The PR is mergeable and awaiting review.

@rjpower I've tried a few flavors of building this deterministically and all of them feel like they add far too much code and complexity for the benefit they'd provide. That said, I have had good luck with just asking an agent where the diffs in a `pulumi preview` are coming from (as you've noticed when I nag you in Slack). So, my suggestion here would be,
1. Create an agent skill that does that, so it's codified ([PR here](https://github.com/marin-community/marin/pull/8649)).
2. (optionally) Run that skill after PR merges that have IaC diffs to provide the reminders. I'd, honestly, lean against this since it's just as easy to run it locally once you see the diff, but since you're the one who would primarily receive the nagging, I'll leave it up to you to decide whether you want it 🙂.
